Let's start with thinking about what would happen if we kept $A$ constant and changed $B$.

We can rewrite the expression as $(B+3)\cdot\left(\dfrac{1}{A^2}\right)$. This way, we see that the expression has two factors, and only one of them depends on $B$.

$\begin{align}
\boxed{B+3}&\cdot\boxed{\dfrac{1}{A^2}}
\\
\nearrow\quad\quad&\phantom{\cdot}\quad\quad\nwarrow
\\
\text{depends on B}\quad&\quad\text{doesn't depend on B}
\end{align}$

What happens to $B+3$ when we increase or decrease $B$'s value?

Only one of the factors depends on $A$:

$\begin{align}
\boxed{B+3}&\cdot\boxed{\dfrac{1}{A^2}}
\\
\nearrow\quad\quad&\phantom{\cdot}\quad\quad\nwarrow
\\
\text{doesn't depend on A}\quad&\quad\text{depends on A}
\end{align}$

When we increase $A$, the value of $A^2$ increases as well. Since $A^2$ is in the denominator, the value of $\dfrac{1}{A^2}$ decreases and so does the value of the overall expression. If we want the expression's value to increase, we need to *decrease* $A$.
